Directory of "Mail:" files:

    Bin/

        - stores all JamMail executable binaries, INCLUDING JamMail itself!

    dupetables/

        - Used to store tables of message id's which are used in catching
          echomail duplicates that come over the line. CNet creates the
          tables as needed. All YOU need to do is create the directory.

    FFRS/

        - Contains files for use with FFRS (Fido File Request Server).

    fido/

        - Contains dos executables, as well as standard ARexx utilities
          which do various things for me.

    inbound/

        - This directory should be created and assigned as INBOUND:. It is
          where all new mail packets, crash-sent files, .TIC files, NodeDiff
          files, etc will be stored.

    jammail/

        - Where I store all other files relating to JamMail(like text files,
          doc files, etc.)

    netmail/

        - If you plan on allowing UUCP type mail to be sent and received,
          then you'll need this directory. Also remember to assign it as
          NETMAIL:

    nodelist/

        - Stores the current NodeList and index files. Is also the place I
          chose to store TrapList and TrapList.cfg, which are really only
          used in conjunction with NodeLists anyways.

    outbound/

        - This directory, which should also be assigned as OUTBOUND: is where
          all messages, netmail, file attaches, etc. that will originate from
          your bbs are placed. Further, JamScan will look in this directory
          when it does it's work to find files it needs to crunch.

    queue/

        - This directory is used by JamMail when performing FREQ requests.
          You need to assign this directory as XFERQ: if you'll be using
          JamMail to process file requests. Don't mess with any of the files
          you may find in this directory yourself.

    temp/

        - The "Poll-Script2" file copies files located in INBOUND:, OUTBOUND:
          and NETMAIL: to the mirror directories inside this base, just in
          case something goes awry. You'll have these files to fall back on.

    users/

        - Needed by CNet Amiga to store user mail directories. This is also
          the directory used to store UUCP mail.

    JamCall.rexx

        - This is the intelligent dialer script I wrote for use with JamMail.
          The "Poll-Script2" file calls this script, as does the "CallJam"
          file, located in the "pfiles:fido/" directory.

    jamscan.cfg

        - Here's the config I'm presently using, just so you'll have another
          example to look at.

    Poll-Script1
    Poll-Script2

        - To call my Boss Node for polling, I use these two scripts. I use
          CNet's Events Scheduler to launch the first one, which in turn
          launches the second one. Numerous things are done with this script
          as shown below:

                - Logs are restarted.
                - Files are backed up.
                - CNet Toss is run to export needed echo packets.
                - Outbound files are JamScanned.
                - My boss node is dialed, upon which anything from my end
                  is sent, and anything from his end is received by me.
                - Files are backed up.
                - NodeDiff's are processed.
                - .TIC files are processed.
                - Echomail packets are tossed(using CNet Toss).
                - Script is exited.

        - If you'd like to use this script yourself, you'll have to do some
          editing within the script to change things like node addresses as
          well as activating certain portions of code if you want to use
          them(I deactivated the NodeDiff and .TIC processors in case you
          don't wish to use them, but you can activate them if you want)

        - Lastly, as mentioned above, I launch the first script through CNet
          twice a day as an event using these settings:

            Command: DOS-CMD
               Args: run >NIL: execute mail:poll-script1
          [port(s)]: 1                 (the port JamMail is loaded on)
               Type: Immediate--force system idle
               Days: Check ALL Seven
               Time: 800               (For 8AM)
              Valid: 100               (Try to poll between 8 and 9)
           Interval: 1200              (Every 12 hours)
            Iterate: 2                 (twice daily at 8AM and 8PM)

